Write a self-contained prompt that instructs an AI coding agent to build a
Python script which scans files for invalid escape sequences in string literals,
optionally auto-fixes them, and reports results.
Describe behavior, inputs, outputs, dependencies, logic, error handling, and
edge cases, without any code or snippets.
Create a Python 3 command-line tool that scans one or more source files for
invalid escape sequences in string literals, reports them, and optionally
repairs them in place.
The script should be runnable under Termux on Android (use a
`#!/data/data/com.termux/files/usr/bin/env python` shebang) and must be a single
self-contained file that uses only the standard library. **Purpose:** Detect and
optionally fix "invalid escape sequence" problems in Python source files, such
as a string like `"\d"` or `"\s"` that Python would otherwise warn about
(`DeprecationWarning`/`SyntaxWarning`) and which will become an error in future
Python versions.
A raw-string or doubled-backslash fix is applied to the offending string
literals. **Command-line interface:** Use `argparse`.
Accept: - One or more positional file paths (or directories, which should be
expanded to the `.py` files they contain, if you choose to support that; at
minimum accept file paths). - A flag (for example `--fix`) that enables in-place
rewriting.
Without it, the script only reports issues and must not modify any file. -
Optionally a flag to choose the number of worker processes; default to a
sensible value based on CPU count. **Parallelism:** Process files concurrently
using `multiprocessing` (a process pool).
Each worker takes a tuple of (file path string, fix flag) and returns a tuple of
(file path string, whether the file was changed, list of human-readable message
strings, and an optional replacement/new-content value).
The main process collects the results, prints messages, and writes out fixed
content when applicable.
Make sure the worker function is top-level so it is picklable. **Per-file logic
(in the worker):** 1.
Read the file as UTF-8 text.
If reading fails, return an error message in red ANSI color (`\033[91m`)
containing the path and exception, reset with `\033[0m`, and report no change.
2.
Compile the source text inside a `warnings.catch_warnings(record=True)` context,
with `SyntaxWarning` set to `"always"`.
Catch `SyntaxError` (record that a syntax error occurred) and any other
exception (ignore it).
Determine whether any recorded warning message contains the text "invalid escape
sequence". 3.
If there was neither an invalid-escape warning nor a syntax error, return that
the file is clean, with no messages and no changes. 4.
Otherwise, tokenize the source with `tokenize.generate_tokens` over a
`io.StringIO` of the text, suppressing `tokenize.TokenError` via
`contextlib.suppress` so partial token lists are still usable. 5.
Iterate over the tokens.
For each `STRING` token, examine the literal text: skip raw strings (prefix
containing `r` or `R`), and for non-raw strings find backslash escape sequences
that are not valid Python escapes (valid ones include newline continuation,
`\\`, quotes, `a b f n r t v`, octal digits `0-7`, `x`, `N`, `u`, `U`).
Use a regex to locate backslash-plus-character sequences, handling bytes
literals (prefix containing `b`/`B`) where `\N`, `\u`, `\U` are not valid
escapes. 6.
For each invalid escape found, record an issue message (with file path, line and
column from the token start, and the offending sequence) in a readable form,
colorized in cyan (`\033[96m`) with reset.
When fixing, build a replacement that doubles the backslash (or converts the
literal to a raw-string prefix, whichever you choose consistently), and record
the replacement with its position. 7.
Apply the replacements to the source text (process them from the end of the file
toward the start, or otherwise ensure offsets remain valid), and return the new
content. **Writing:** When `--fix` is given and the file changed, write the new
content back as UTF-8 in the main process, and print a cyan success line naming
the file.
When not fixing, print the issues so the user can see them and optionally a hint
to rerun with the fix flag.
Print a final summary of how many files had issues and how many were fixed.
**Output and exit status:** Print issue lines and errors to standard output or
standard error consistently.
Exit with status 0 when no issues are found (or all were fixed) and a non-zero
status when issues remain unfixed or any file could not be read.
Handle `KeyboardInterrupt` gracefully by terminating the pool and exiting with a
non-zero status. **Edge cases to handle:** - Files that are already clean: skip
quickly with no output. - Files with syntax errors unrelated to escapes:
tokenization may fail partway; still process the tokens gathered so far, and do
not corrupt the file. - Multi-line triple-quoted strings: compute line and
column correctly from the token's start position. - f-strings and prefixed
strings (`f`, `rb`, `Rb`, `BR`, etc.): handle prefix detection
case-insensitively. - Files that are not valid UTF-8: report as an error for
that file only, without stopping the others. - Empty input list: print usage or
a message and exit cleanly. - Never write to a file unless `--fix` is set and
the content actually changed. **Style:** Use `from __future__ import
annotations`, module-level ANSI color constants, concise docstrings or comments
only where helpful, and a `main()` function guarded by `if __name__ ==
"__main__":`.
Keep the code readable and idiomatic, with no third-party dependencies. ---
